## Why This Question Is Asked
This evaluates practical controls for vendor-integrated cloud data access and cross-service trust boundaries.

## Key Concepts
- PKCE and short-lived tokens prevent token replay and limit exposure windows
- Token binding and mTLS establish bidirectional trust between services
- Dynamic credential rotation minimizes the blast radius of leaked secrets
- Policy-as-code IAM checks enable automated, repeatable security enforcement
- Incident response playbooks ensure rapid containment and recovery

## Code Example
```javascript
const verifyPKCE = (codeChallenge, codeVerifier, method = 'S256') => {
  if (method === 'S256') {
    const hash = crypto.createHash('sha256')
      .update(codeVerifier)
      .digest('base64url');
    return hash === codeChallenge;
  }
  return codeVerifier === codeChallenge;
};

const validateTokenBinding = (token, clientCert, deviceFingerprint) => {
  return token.binding.certThumbprint === clientCert.thumbprint
    && token.binding.deviceId === deviceFingerprint.id;
};
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates a candidate's ability to design concrete, layered security controls for a common integration risk in SaaS platforms. It tests understanding of both transport-layer and application-layer protections.

## Key Concepts
- Webhook signature verification using HMAC
- Replay protection via timestamps and narrow validation windows
- Secret management and rotation policies
- Transport security with mutual TLS (mTLS)

## Code Example
```javascript
const crypto = require('crypto');

function verifyWebhookSignature(payload, secret, signature, timestamp, windowMs = 300000) {
  const now = Date.now();
  if (now - timestamp > windowMs) return false;
  const data = \`${timestamp}.${payload}\`;
  const expected = crypto.createHmac('sha256', secret).update(data).digest('hex');
  return crypto.timingSafeEqual(Buffer.from(signature), Buffer.from(expected));
}
```

